<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-21220] socklnd: nonblk_zcack=0 oopses in ksocknal_queue_tx_msg_v2</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21220</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Setting the ksocklnd parameter nonblk_zcack=0 crashes a node within seconds of bulk traffic, with a NULL pointer write in ksocknal_queue_tx_msg_v2() (socklnd_proto.c:134 on master 7c3c9f8814). The default, nonblk_zcack=1, never reaches the code, so only sites that change the parameter are hit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cause: with nonblk_zcack=0 a ZC-ACK that is waiting on a connection as a NOOP is carried by the next LNet message queued there, which should take the NOOP's place in the transmit queue. That replacement used to be list_add() of the new tx after the NOOP followed by list_del() of the NOOP. &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-9679" title="Prepare lustre for adoption into the linux kernel" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-9679"&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-9679&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; ("various: use list_splice and list_splice_init", 45a64335d0, 2.13.53) turned it into list_splice(&amp;amp;tx-&amp;gt;tx_list, &amp;amp;tx_msg-&amp;gt;tx_list), which treats the NOOP's list entry as a list head and splices into the new message's tx_list, which was never initialised. The other conversions in that patch operate on real list heads and are correct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reproduction: two nodes on socklnd, nonblk_zcack=0 on both, lnet_selftest brw of 1 MB transfers. Seen on Rocky 10 VMs (6.12 kernel): oops within seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fix: list_replace(&amp;amp;tx-&amp;gt;tx_list, &amp;amp;tx_msg-&amp;gt;tx_list).&lt;/p&gt;</description>
